From: bakhodirovxz <bohodirovasadbek1@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 20:18:02 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] ui-egui: scroll the page with a one-finger drag on a
 touch screen

On a touch screen, dragging a finger across the page extended a text
selection, so a phone user could not scroll a document by swiping. The
press now waits until it is clear what the finger does:

- it moves further than the touch slop: the page scrolls with the finger
  and keeps coasting (the existing touchpad Momentum) after a flick;
- it lifts: the press is carried out where the finger landed, so a tap
  places the caret;
- it rests for max_click_duration: the press is carried out as a
  double-click, so a long press selects the word (and opens the context
  menu), and the drag after it extends the selection by words;
- a second finger (pinch zoom) leaves the press to the existing handling.

A finger is told from a mouse by egui's Event::Touch, which the web runner
sends alongside the pointer events. egui reports a press to the widget one
frame late for touches, so the press is taken from the input, and a slow
frame may hold a whole gesture at once. Mouse behaviour is unchanged. A
scrolling finger does not take the keyboard focus through the drag it
causes. Read Mode and dialogs are left alone.

The canvas gets touch-action: none, so Chrome does not start its own pan
and cancel the touch.
